Why People Choose the Alternatives (When & Why SQLite Might Struggle)
The reason developers often reach for PostgreSQL or MongoDB comes down to specific scale and operational needs:

1. PostgreSQL (When You Outgrow SQLite Relational Needs)
Concurrent Writes: SQLite locks the database file during writes. If you have hundreds of users writing data simultaneously (e.g., thousands of active checkouts per minute), SQLite can trigger database is locked errors. PostgreSQL manages concurrent row-level writes cleanly.

Horizontal & Multi-Server Scaling: If you scale your Express backend to run across 3 separate server instances or container replicas, they can't easily share a local SQLite file without complex volume syncing or LiteFS. PostgreSQL sits on its own network server, allowing any number of backend servers to connect to it over TCP.

Advanced Features: Row-Level Security (RLS), complex JSON indexing (jsonb with GIN indexes), and rich enum types.  ￼
Jam SQL Studio

2. MongoDB (When You Want Document-Shaped Data)
Unstructured/Dynamic Schemas: MongoDB excels when every record might have completely different fields or deeply nested sub-documents (e.g., user profiles with highly variable settings or custom metadata) without needing fixed SQL schemas or table migrations.  ￼
JusDB

Native JSON Alignment: JavaScript objects match BSON/JSON directly.

Recommendation for Your Project
Stick with SQLite!

Unless you plan to deploy your backend across multiple load-balanced servers on day one or expect thousands of simultaneous write operations, SQLite (via packages like better-sqlite3 or sqlite3) will be faster, easier to maintain, and completely free of dev friction. You can always migrate to PostgreSQL later if you reach massive traffic!  ￼
