Gemini said
No, this schema does not make your site less secure by default.

In fact, linking multiple authentication methods to a single email address is the standard pattern used by platforms like Google, Spotify, and GitHub itself.

However, allowing both password and GitHub logins on the same account introduces three specific security risks that you must account for in your backend code.

1. The "Unverified GitHub Email" Hijack Risk (Critical)
The Threat
Anyone can create a GitHub account with any email address. If GitHub hasn't verified that the user actually owns that email address, an attacker could:

Create a fake GitHub account using your admin’s email address (admin@yourcompany.com).

Click "Sign in with GitHub" on your website.

If your backend automatically links the GitHub account based on email alone, the attacker instantly gains access to the admin account without ever knowing the password.

The Fix
When receiving user details back from GitHub's OAuth API, only trust emails where email_verified: true.

JavaScript
￼
￼
// Example check in your Express OAuth handler
if (!githubUser.email || !githubUser.email_verified) {
  return res.status(400).json({ 
    error: "Your GitHub account email must be verified to log in." 
  });
}
2. Password Account Takeover via Social Login
The Threat
If a user created an account via GitHub OAuth, their password_hash in SQLite is NULL. If an attacker later targets the standard email/password endpoint and tries to trigger a password reset or exploit a weak validation check, they might set a password on an account the real owner intended to be GitHub-only.

The Fix
In your Login and Password Reset endpoints, explicitly handle NULL passwords:

Login Endpoint: If user.password_hash === null, return a message like: "This account uses GitHub Sign-In. Please log in using GitHub."

Password Reset Endpoint: Do not allow password resets for accounts with password_hash IS NULL unless they first verify ownership via an email confirmation link.

3. Account Link Lockout (OAuth Provider Revocation)
The Threat
If a developer relies only on GitHub login (password_hash is NULL) and loses access to their GitHub account (or GitHub experiences an outage), they are locked out of your system with no fallback.

The Fix
Prompt GitHub-only users inside their dashboard/portal settings to set up a local backup password if they wish, populating the password_hash column.

Summary Security Checklist
Threat	Risk Level	Simple Solution
Unverified GitHub Email	🔴 High	Reject OAuth logins if email_verified is false from GitHub.
Null Password Bypasses	🟡 Medium	Reject password logins if password_hash is NULL.
Account Lockout	🟢 Low	Allow users to add a backup password in profile settings.
￼
As long as you enforce email verification from GitHub before merging accounts, your schema is secure.

