CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS invitations (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
  email TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
  assigned_role TEXT CHECK(assigned_role IN ('developer', 'admin', 'superuser')) NOT NULL,
  token TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,       -- Secret invite token generated by backend
  created_by INTEGER,               -- User ID of the admin who created the invite
  is_used INTEGER DEFAULT 0,
  created_at DATETIME DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
  FOREIGN KEY (created_by) REFERENCES users(id)
);

-- 2. Users Table
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
  email TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
  password_hash TEXT,
  github_id TEXT UNIQUE,
  github_username TEXT,
  role TEXT CHECK(role IN ('user', 'developer', 'admin', 'superuser')) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'user',
  is_active INTEGER DEFAULT 1,
  created_at DATETIME DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
  
  CHECK (password_hash IS NOT NULL OR github_id IS NOT NULL)
);
2. How the Invitation Rules Work
Scenario A: Standard Customer (role = 'user')
Anyone can sign up via the public portal (if public registration is enabled for customers).

The backend automatically forces their role to 'user' on creation.

Scenario B: GitHub OAuth Login (Strict Lockdown)
When a user clicks "Sign in with GitHub":

Express fetches their verified email from GitHub.

The backend queries SQLite:

Case 1: Email exists in users table → Log them in seamlessly.

Case 2: Email exists in invitations table (and is_used = 0) → Create their account, assign the role specified in the invite, mark invitations.is_used = 1, and log them in.

Case 3: Email is NOT in users AND NOT in invitations → REJECT IMMEDIATELY.

403 Forbidden: "Your GitHub email (email@domain.com) is not authorized. Access is invitation-only."
Scenario C: Invited Registration via Email/Password
An Admin sends an invite link containing a unique token:

http://localhost:5173/portal.html?invite_token=abc123xyz

The developer opens the link. The backend checks if token is valid and unused in invitations.

If valid, the registration form unlocks, allows them to set a password, creates the account with the invited role, and burns the token (is_used = 1).

3. Seed / Bootstrap Script for the First Superuser
Because the site is locked down, how do you get the very first Admin or Superuser account in?

We add a quick bootstrap check in backend/server.js when the server starts. If no superuser exists, it generates a pre-approved invitation or creates a master superuser from environment variables.

JavaScript
￼
