You do not need cors if your backend and frontend are served together or proxied, and you only need speakeasy + qrcode if you choose to enforce 2FA (TOTP authenticator apps).

Here is exactly what each package does and whether you can remove it to keep your project lean and simple:

1. cors (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing)
What it does: Allows a frontend running on a different domain/port (e.g. http://localhost:5173) to make API calls to your backend (e.g. http://localhost:5000).

Can you remove it? YES, if you use Vite’s proxy.

How to eliminate it: Configure Vite to proxy /api requests directly to your Express server in vite.config.js. Once set up, the browser sees both frontend and backend on http://localhost:5173, making CORS completely unnecessary.

JavaScript
￼
￼
// vite.config.js
export default defineConfig({
  root: 'frontend',
  server: {
    proxy: {
      '/api': 'http://localhost:5000'
    }
  }
});
Security Impact: Removing cors and using single-origin proxying actually improves security by keeping API ports hidden from public browser exposure.

2. speakeasy & qrcode
What they do: speakeasy generates and verifies 6-digit authenticator app tokens (Google Authenticator, 1Password, Authy). qrcode renders the QR code image for users to scan on set up.

Can you remove them? YES, if you rely on GitHub OAuth for 2FA.

The Simpler Alternative: Developers and admins already have 2FA enabled on their GitHub accounts. If you delegate elevated login to GitHub OAuth, GitHub handles the 2FA enforcement for you. You don't need to build, store, or verify TOTP keys in your own code at all!

The Stripped-Down, Zero-Fluff Stack
By leveraging Vite's proxy and GitHub OAuth, you can drop cors, speakeasy, and qrcode entirely.

