It is completely normal and standard. In fact, import { defineConfig } from 'vite'; is the official, recommended way to write any Vite configuration file.

Here is why it's used and what it actually does under the hood:

What defineConfig Does
defineConfig is a lightweight helper function provided directly by Vite.

At runtime, it simply returns whatever configuration object you pass into it:

JavaScript
￼
￼
// What Vite's source code basically looks like:
export function defineConfig(config) {
  return config;
}
It doesn't transform your configuration or add any hidden magic.

Why It Exists
Auto-Completion & Type Checking (Intellisense):
Without defineConfig, your code editor (like VS Code or WebStorm) doesn't know what properties are valid inside a plain JavaScript object. By wrapping your config in defineConfig(...), TypeScript/JSDoc types kick in automatically—giving you auto-complete for properties like server, proxy, plugins, and build.

Cross-Format Compatibility:
It allows Vite to seamlessly parse both .js, .mjs, and .ts configuration files without needing special transpilation flags.

Example Comparison
Standard / Recommended (With defineConfig)
JavaScript
￼
￼
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  server: {
    port: 5173,
    proxy: {
      '/api': 'http://localhost:5000',
    },
  },
});
Without defineConfig (Also valid, but no autocomplete)
JavaScript
￼
￼
export default {
  server: {
    port: 5173,
    proxy: {
      '/api': 'http://localhost:5000',
    },
  },
};
Both execute identically in Vite, but using import { defineConfig } from 'vite'; prevents typos and gives you full editor support when tweaking options like proxies or dev server ports.

