You have to run nix-shell -p nodejs_22 --run "npm run dev" instead of just npm run dev because of how NixOS / Nix package manager handles software environments.

Here is the exact reason it happens, along with a simple fix so you can type npm run dev normally again.

1. Why npm run dev Fails on its Own
On traditional Linux distributions (like Ubuntu, Debian, or macOS), when you install Node.js, its executables (node and npm) are placed into global directories like /usr/bin or /usr/local/bin, which are permanently added to your system's $PATH.

Nix works differently:

Nix does not dump programs into /usr/bin.

Everything lives isolated inside the Nix Store (/nix/store/...).

Unless Node.js has been explicitly installed into your user profile or activated inside an ephemeral shell, your system terminal has no idea where npm or node are located.

When you run nix-shell -p nodejs_22 --run "...":

Nix spins up a temporary sub-shell.

It fetches Node.js v22 and adds it to that sub-shell's $PATH.

It executes your command (npm run dev).

Once done, the sub-shell exits, and npm vanishes from your terminal's PATH again.

2. How to Fix It (So You Can Just Type npm run dev)
You have two clean ways to make npm run dev work directly:

Option A: Install Node.js permanently into your user environment (Global)
If you want npm and node to always be available in every terminal session on your machine, install it to your profile:

Bash
￼
￼
nix-env -iA nixos.nodejs_22
Once installed, close and reopen your terminal. You can now type:

Bash
￼
￼
npm run dev
Option B: Create a shell.nix file in your project (Best Practice)
If you work on multiple projects with different Node.js versions, creating a shell.nix file in your project root is the standard Nix developer workflow.

Create a file named shell.nix in /jobs/LowEx/website/:

Nix
￼
￼
{ pkgs ? import <nixpkgs> {} }:

pkgs.mkShell {
  buildInputs = [
    pkgs.nodejs_22
  ];
}
Now, whenever you open your terminal, just run:

Bash
￼
￼
nix-shell
This enters an interactive Nix development environment with npm and node enabled. Inside that shell, you can simply run:

Bash
￼
￼
npm run dev
Comparison Summary
Method	What you type	Pros	Cons
nix-shell -p nodejs_22 --run ...	Long command	One-liner, nothing saved to disk	Repetitive to type every time
nix-env -iA nixos.nodejs_22	npm run dev	Works everywhere like standard Linux	Can cause version conflicts across projects
shell.nix	nix-shell then npm run dev	Standard Nix practice, keeps project pinned to Node 22	Requires typing nix-shell once per terminal session
